Hmm.
This whole business seems to be fraught with difficulties. As has been
mentioned, in real life these different weapons were used for
different circumstances. Close combat, different armour types, and so
on. But in nethack there are no different armour types, and the only
variables we have available for tweaking are weight, damage vs big,
and damage vs small. I suppose short swords could be given a to-hit
bonus or something, but with the current balance that would hardly
make any difference anyway.

There could be whole new game mechanics added, such as different
armour types and different weapon speeds, but I don't really want to
make things more complicated than they already are. (Although
different weapon speeds seems like a pretty good feature to include in
a game like this.)

There are a couple of reasons why I chose to group longsword & two-
handed rather than long & short & broad, but they aren't particularly
strong reasons. Firstly let me just remind us that I'm not talking
about merging the skills, but rather just 'grouping' them. And just
for aesthetic reasons, I'd prefer to group stuff in pairs where
possible. Ok.. so onto the reasons: From what I understand, a
'longsword' typically refers to what we might call a 1.5 handed sword,
or a bastard sword =96 it can be wielded either one handed or two
handed. I haven't bothered to do any research on what a 'two-handed
sword' might mean, but I assume it can only be wielded with two hands.
Meanwhile, a shortsword is only ever one-handed, and although a
broadsword is a vague term, it is probably a one-handed sword as well.
So, Doug, I agree that it's natural to group them based on the number
of hands, but I'd lean towards putting the longsword with the two-
handed sword because I imagine it to be often wielded with two hands.
I feel this decision is strengthened a little by the 'axes' skill,
which includes one-handed axes and two-handed axes.

I'm actually a bit tempted to tweak some of the 1.5 handed weapons (eg
longswords) so that they do slightly less damage when dual-wielding,
to reflect the fact that you might sometimes use the sword with two-
hands for a big attack. But I won't do that yet.

Incidentally, yesterday I noticed that the broadswords in nethack
weighs 70 units. A longsword only weighs 40, so 70 just seems crazy to
me. I feel that I should dramatically reduce it down to around 40. I
don't know much about swords, but when I see stuff for sale like this:
http://www.medievalware.com/Sir-William-Marshall-Sword-p/2000cs.htm
I'm led to believe that a broadsword wouldn't weigh twice as much as a
longsword...


